Introduction
************

   The `Zebra' supports BGP-4, RIP-1,2 and will support OSPFv2, RIPng
and so on.  Zebra has one daemon for each protocols.  For changing
kernel routing table and for redistribution between different routing
protocols, it has a zebra daemon.  So zebra is name of a routing daemon
as well as the name of the routing software package name.  There is no
need for these daemon to be running over the same machine.  This allows
for the possibility of a new formation of the routing software.

     SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE:
     
       +----+  +----+  +-----+  +-----+
       |BGPd|  |RIPd|  |OSPFd|  |zebra|
       +----+  +----+  +-----+  +-----+
                                  |
       +--------------------------|---+
       |                          v   |
       |    UNIX Kernel  routing table|
       +------------------------------+

   Zebra use a multi-thread mechanism when it runs under a multi-thread
supported kernel.  It will also run under traditional UNIX kernels such
as FreeBSD, Solaris, Linux.  When zebra runs under GNU Hurd kernel it
will act as a kernel routing table because there is no traditional
kernel routing table present.  Under GNU hurd all TCP/IP services are
provided by user processes such as `pflocal' or `pfinet' server.  Zebra
will provide all the routing selection mechanisms for those processes.

   BGPd will also act as Route Server and Route Reflector.  BGPd can
dump both BGP-4 updates and withdraw message.  BGPd acts as Route
Server which exchang routing information. When you only need Route
Server or Route Reflector, there is no need of running RIPd, OSPFd, and
zebra daemon.

   This will allow there will be new potential of routing software by
dividing protocol module to different daemon.


